! {{ ansible_managed }}
!
! Tenant egress: a default route inside each tenant VRF, pointing at the
! perimeter over the transit segment.
!
! WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
!
! Proxmox generates `default-originate ipv4|ipv6` under
! `router bgp <asn> vrf vrf_<tenant>`, which ADVERTISES a default to other
! VTEPs over EVPN. It installs nothing in the exit node's own VRF. On a
! single-member fabric the only node IS the exit node, so there is no peer to
! learn from and the VRF holds only its connected route.
!
! Proxmox's own answer to that is in EvpnPlugin.pm: on an exit node it deletes
! the VRF's `unreachable default`, so a lookup that misses falls through to the
! MAIN table. That does give a tenant the internet - and it also sends
! tenant traffic bound for the management segment straight out vmbr0, on-link,
! unSNATed, never touching the perimeter. Measured: five tenant-sourced packets
! captured on the management VLAN with source 10.20.129.10.
!
! An explicit default inside the VRF is what closes that. Every destination the
! VRF does not connect to leaves via the transit gateway, so the perimeter sees
! all of it and ADR-0001 seam 1 holds literally rather than approximately.
! This is policy, not a single-member workaround: the exit node needs it however
! many members the fabric has.
!
! Proxmox merges this file into the config it generates
! (PVE/Network/SDN/Frr.pm), and its parser folds a bare `vrf <name>` stanza
! into the generated block rather than duplicating it - so these lines land
! inside Proxmox's own `vrf vrf_<tenant>` section on every SDN apply.
{% for tenant in proxmox_tenant_egress.tenants %}

vrf vrf_{{ tenant }}
 ip route 0.0.0.0/0 {{ _transit_gateway }} nexthop-vrf default
exit-vrf
{% endfor %}
